# The Classic `for` Loop 🔁

The `for` loop executes a block of code a predetermined number of times:

```javascript
// for (initialization; condition; increment/decrement)
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  console.log(`Iteration: ${i}`);
}
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  console.log(`Iteration: ${i}`);
}

// Counting down
for (let count = 3; count > 0; count--) {
  console.log(`Countdown: ${count}`);
}
console.log("Blast off!");
FULL_CODE_END -->

## 3 Expressions:
1. `let i = 0`: Executed once before loop starts.
2. `i < 5`: Condition evaluated before each iteration.
3. `i++`: Executed at the end of each iteration.

# Iterating Over Arrays with `for` 📊

Traverse an array using index notation and `.length`:

```javascript
const fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry", "Mango"];

for (let i = 0; i < fruits.length; i++) {
  console.log(`Index ${i}: ${fruits[i]}`);
}
```

